    {
      "title": "Facts and statements",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "NICE NG71 recommends leaving well-tolerated hallucinations and delusions untreated; potentially triggering Parkinson medicines should be reduced only with specialist advice and consideration of symptom severity and withdrawal effects. (low confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-27)",
          "source": "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng71/chapter/recommendations",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:3b5096c0c28598f3210a7919389ea526e24d9faf6006321b9d5f0d295c7e2d88",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:6e0b95b4985ee140636b6a15f8382c13492c6bcc7522f092d6465f2579b4976e"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The 2019 MDS review rated clozapine and pimavanserin clinically useful for PD psychosis; the 2024 systematic review of reviews also identified these two agents as having the strongest efficacy and tolerability evidence. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6916382/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:2707858f2a65b46919d2182ea4ed0edca7d434165ae86d52522f9d90de7fee9b",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:029df432cd9f2b28279359aaf85bbed75511dc4a388210d24c4fb9bb67343d57",
            "ev:1a1e27a19ff2b84d0d90119ef4c216ea88fade211872b0754e51718c1e356e87",
            "ev:3f93107b2208d5c1b73b683cb866089dca482fb49dc16dd739f86f10b69faa33",
            "ev:e13ad55b54a0be839d837f1d18a21f0e98e0d5a42fab2a24c7ba7dc1231d6237"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11505035/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Quetiapine should not be described as having established placebo-controlled efficacy for PD psychosis: the 2019 MDS review rated evidence insufficient and practice implications possibly useful; NICE NG71 nevertheless says to consider it in patients without cognitive impairment. (medium confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-27)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6916382/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:b6dc9f66046bb2825bbf3217c63e9e59488740c6db881ddfefcca15c2301bfc6",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:3f93107b2208d5c1b73b683cb866089dca482fb49dc16dd739f86f10b69faa33",
            "ev:4d430e4c520423c959e1173e399471f8dba88e5ae789d91b496ce69a533548c7",
            "ev:5e2e6b61813cdcf6d8c9f6288454f4ca906d316fef4e8675d42961732e602d47"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng71/chapter/recommendations"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CBT has supportive evidence for PD depression: the 2019 MDS review rated it likely efficacious and possibly useful, while a 2024 meta-analysis found reductions in depression and anxiety; blinding constraints and the need for longer follow-up qualify confidence. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6916382/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:c1de6f54d12e2c22da650bcfe5d84cfd65d152eb8044914b45afb7bb50450655",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:3f93107b2208d5c1b73b683cb866089dca482fb49dc16dd739f86f10b69faa33",
            "ev:83f31899cb717cee918345be164503640806e3383e15a1b43952168db3d7dacc",
            "ev:abbcce798dadba3f42e52350063b4eca123c499f720aed5fed908df4779300af",
            "ev:f9376d14973f4105b9ede494588fc4adcc932a2e2c2378f1c9752041dd7ca51c"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11470855/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "In the 48-participant anxiety CBT trial, CBT did not significantly outperform clinical monitoring on the primary Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale endpoint, although it improved the PD-specific Parkinson Anxiety Scale; benefit should be described as outcome-dependent. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9290129/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:73b8fd7c02156d0e5b35d983cb84cfd027971ac48cb4613d9b398dd55654581a",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:0c126744838a8b43accb0fd016e4417d031948e42ae5d406ca470c9ed093a150",
            "ev:2ec557cf0d32e1dc34a127ea4e63f3e6d201ac4371051faf051b8bfd4eddcc6e",
            "ev:6b4dcd225c7d28ad686128f662aae5dfdbfd9ec5b5a07932c30eb76545d42897",
            "ev:db335d8d29562745ed11044a4acffc5c8762b7d1d072059317e056181e3011c7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The absence of eligible anxiety trials in the 2019 MDS review is a historical search result, not a current claim that no PD anxiety RCTs exist: a later randomized CBT study reported both a nonsignificant general anxiety endpoint and benefit on a PD-specific scale. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6916382/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:6743b1af2f47984eb8adc72b6da0cab687d0b55add7f3316e9ffcdef81170df0",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:0c126744838a8b43accb0fd016e4417d031948e42ae5d406ca470c9ed093a150",
            "ev:27a678941e3e5fd1ddd76d59e1a3d4b27422524c05da8c1cbd29d0352f9e2bcf",
            "ev:2ec557cf0d32e1dc34a127ea4e63f3e6d201ac4371051faf051b8bfd4eddcc6e",
            "ev:3f93107b2208d5c1b73b683cb866089dca482fb49dc16dd739f86f10b69faa33",
            "ev:8b788060596fe80baea9b598951299aa72275d3712a8f92d96656579f119a3ec"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9290129/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Apathy drug evidence is restricted: the 2019 MDS review based rivastigmine possibly-useful status on one small positive study, and piribedil possibly-useful status applied specifically after subthalamic stimulation and dopamine-agonist withdrawal. These findings should not be generalized to every patient with apathy. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6916382/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:0b8b0e51c8b563f6187e92a3b32fe978451ab9cd169ac287515d8a2a45ff0f1b",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:3f93107b2208d5c1b73b683cb866089dca482fb49dc16dd739f86f10b69faa33",
            "ev:c521b55c0941324f8384738a2a5fde9f2f81ff756e16d64b9590c2f0608c6fb6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A small retrospective psychosis cohort found no statistically significant mortality difference between pimavanserin and quetiapine; retrospective design, mixed PD/Lewy-body-dementia enrollment, concomitant treatment and limited power prevent concluding equivalent mortality safety. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7061324/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:139d24982d215ef57fcc13c75fbefa7a04b9c05b72fa7fba9b8cbf9c7a890385",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:86918dcd5ba268bcc38fb3b1ae7dfd67f10f9a2536f370d4f68370b58963b939",
            "ev:d3b96b9b6bbf21a82352fe458ed964bc4fd54c6b12c24e85a1226074873304ae",
            "ev:e56a93bb7e424d63dd24a5d32df486c76dbfbe3ca0c247a4747291bc892cf519"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Apathy is reduced motivation and goal-directed thinking or action; it can occur independently of depression. The 2024 German guideline stresses that distinguishing apathy from depressive affect is clinically challenging. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11561078/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:97f1a73aa9c1d7814bc1cfb1178956a0a74485a566f899d354b02230ca50637d",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:2ba8a8a117602d8157b4d9475fe83281d6c784250fc2305e49105793c1aabf92",
            "ev:2c61bffbba7abef5cdf38b3d4e257f4120def6247be61329bdcb2171a282ddae",
            "ev:869c6a30c98872fa6f037e771169bd1c0f56625627221f3680030330d683ce43"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pimavanserin improved PD psychosis in the placebo-controlled phase 3 trial without worsening motor function. The 2019 MDS review judged short-term efficacy sufficient for a clinically useful rating, while explicitly noting the limited controlled safety evidence beyond six weeks available to that review. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24183563/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:1f173dc6ccbf8258ca88da429a7f7fcec565cad52e6a51f530c53a61d8b71d5b",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:029df432cd9f2b28279359aaf85bbed75511dc4a388210d24c4fb9bb67343d57",
            "ev:3f93107b2208d5c1b73b683cb866089dca482fb49dc16dd739f86f10b69faa33",
            "ev:4a509753bf104f56651f0f8fe4d62e102606e85f19c403456e41a9f806b0170c",
            "ev:d58e054b90eaed4adc1ace03f25c1e2ebe7b784e8389676779649c71653548fd"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6916382/"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CBT is supported for depressive symptoms in PD. The 2019 MDS review rated it likely efficacious and possibly useful while noting blinding and adverse-event-reporting limitations; the 2024 German guideline recommends offering CBT. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6916382/",
          "note": null,
